  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1245 PM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nTemperatures fall below climatology (highs below 70 F) to kick off \nthe long term period as heights decrease slightly behind the cold \nfront. The upper-level trough digs south Friday night into Saturday. \nLift from this feature and its associated jet max bring chances \n(generally 20-60%) of scattered showers and storms through Sunday. \nThe best chances (40-60%) look to be south of I-10, though a couple \nof showers/storms may develop to the north. However, it should be \nnoted there is much uncertainty in rainfall amounts/coverage due to \nmodel discrepancies regrading the placement of the trough. \n\nIncreasing cloud cover in addition to surface high pressure over the \nCentral Plains filters cooler air into the region this weekend, \nyielding highs in the 50s and 60s. Saturday looks to be the coolest \nday in the forecast as highs dip into the 50s for most. Some frost \nmay be possible on cooler nights across the higher terrain of the \nGuadalupes, Marfa Plateau, and northern Lea County. By early next \nweek, long-range guidance has an upper ridge building over the \nregion, bringing warmer and drier conditions.\n\nGreening",
